  "run": "b3c2cb92881d",
  "submission": 1,
  "model": "muse-spark-1.3",
  "question": "Why did well F-12 decline faster after 2010? Compare with the 2005 development plan."
 },
 "to_review": false,
 "note": "Live checker rejected Findings 1-2 for '9.000' and '0,80' (Norwegian number formats in PUD quotes: false rejections, since fixed) and 'due to' inside a quote (the report's claim, not the finding's). Only the summary's unhedged cause ('driving') is a real problem.",
 "expected": {
  "accepted": false,
  "flagged": [
   "Summary"
  ]
 },
 "submission": {
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "The 2005 PUD planned field plateau rate of 9,000 Sm3/d for about 19 months — 'Platåraten på 9.000 Sm3/d vil vare om lag 19 måneder' — and stated production would then fall with rising water: 'Produksjonen avtar grunnet økende vannrate' / 'Production declines due to increasing water rate'.",
    "confidence": "fact",
    "evidence_ids": [
     "E7"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "The PUD predicted 'injeksjonsvannet som bryter gjennom i brønnene etter omlag 2 år' / 'Injection water breaks through in the wells after about 2 years', with design limit 'Maksimum vannkutt 0,80' / 'Maximum water cut is 0.80' and economic rate 'minimum 2100 Sm3/d' / quoted 'minimum 2100 Sm3/d'.",
    "confidence": "fact",
    "evidence_ids": [
     "E3",
     "E2"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "F-12, first oil 2008-02-12, first exceeded 5% water cut on 2009-10-31 (median 0.05795, 20.6 months after first oil), then shifted 0.029 to 0.513 around 2010-02-14 and first exceeded 0.5 on 2010-06-04 (median 0.5011, 27.69 months after first oil) — on-time versus the ~2-year plan.",
    "confidence": "fact",
    "evidence_ids": [
     "E1",
     "E4"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Monthly data show water cut 0.0988 in 2009-12 rising to 0.5446 in 2010-06, 0.5975 in 2010-07 and 0.7112 in 2010-12, while oil rate per producing day fell from 5205 Sm3/d in 2009-12 to 1464 Sm3/d in 2010-12, a change of -71.87%.",
    "confidence": "fact",
    "evidence_ids": [
     "E5",
     "E9"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Oil-rate regime change around 2010-07-18 dropped weekly mean from 4050 to 1146 Sm3/d (-72%), followed by exponential decline from 2010-07-18 to 2012-12-09 with qi 1798 Sm3/producing-day, di 0.4461 per year, r2_log 0.8732 with no warnings, then a second shift 1146 to 383.91 Sm3/d around 2012-12-09.",
    "confidence": "fact",
    "evidence_ids": [
     "E4",
     "E6"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Missing BHP after 2010-10-12 is a gauge failure, not reservoir collapse: bhp_bar reads 0 on 2095 days to 2016-09-17 while flowing 1830 days producing 1133053 Sm3 oil; last good 253.85 bar on 2010-10-11, with monthly BHP null from 2010-11 onward.",
    "confidence": "fact",
    "evidence_ids": [
     "E4",
     "E5"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "F-12 later exceeded the planned 0.80 water-cut limit, first exceeding 0.8 on 2012-02-14 (median 0.8008, 48.06 months after first oil) after shifting 0.5134 to 0.8141 around 2010-10-24, reaching 0.8897 in 2012-12 and 0.8936 in 2013-01, while oil rate fell below the 2100 Sm3/d economic rate (e.g. 1444 in 2011-01, 1464 in 2010-12). Cumulative 4579610 Sm3 oil vs 6833792 Sm3 water gives ratio 1.49.",
    "confidence": "fact",
    "evidence_ids": [
     "E4",
     "E5",
     "E1",
     "E10",
     "E2",
     "E3"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "The faster post-2010 decline was therefore water-breakthrough displacement as foreseen by the PUD, not a shut-in or pressure event: timing matched the ~2-year forecast and the PUD mechanism of rising water rate, but water-cut rise to >80% and oil rates below economic rate show severity beyond the 0.80 facility design.",
    "confidence": "inference",
    "evidence_ids": [
     "E4",
     "E7",
     "E3",
     "E6"
    ]
   }
  ],
  "summary": "F-12 water broke through on schedule (~21 months vs ~2 years planned) then surged past 50% by mid-2010, collapsing oil rate ~72% and driving exponential decline at 0.45/year. This matches the PUD mechanism that production falls with rising water, but F-12 later exceeded the 0.80 design water cut and fell below economic rate. Missing BHP after Oct 2010 is a sensor failure."
 },
